  //  Conductance and its reciprocal

  As promised, let's connect points A and B with a piece of extant matter.
  Let's temporarily assume that nothing prevents the electric field from traveling from A to B.    [a]

  /*
    Important clarification:
      As we connect A and B, the electric charge will travel from A to B -- not the electric charge carriers.
      This is why we call their movement a drift. Electrons will slightly glide in the direction of B until their kinetic energy is
      fully dissipated by the friction they encounter (more on that right away).

      They don't travel in queue or fall sequentially like dominos. Imagine they're balls in a pipe.
      You push the ball at one extremity, all balls move at once.
      If one falls off of the tube, it will be the one on the opposite end of the tube.
      Like the balls in the pipe, electrons all move at once.
  */

  A difference in electric potential tends towards a flow of negative charge through matter.
  How easily this flow happens depends on the composition of said matter.
  That is: each material has the property of letting electrons drift more or less easily. This property is called its conductivity.
  The physical quantity is the Conductance. Its usual symbol is G.
  (If you happen to know why, please let me know... perhaps C, D, E, F were already taken and Greeks have no 'C'?)

  As its conductance decreases, a material is increasingly resisting the flow of electric charge. This property is called its resistivity.
  The physical quantity is the Resistance. Its usual symbol is R.
  (This one's pretty obvious.)

  Don't let the dichotomy fool you: nothing binary here.
  Like autism or dissonance, Resistance is a spectrum.
  Conductance and Resistance are both extremities of one same axis. Extreme cases are:
    #  Conductance = infinity   &&    Resistance = zero
    #   Resistance = infinity   &&   Conductance = zero
  
  /*
    In calculus (integration / differentiation) extreme cases are called limits: we can observe what happens when a variable
    tends to (approaches) an extreme case (limit) but if it actually reaches it (equality), the result is undefined.
    These concepts are often implicit and expressed by equalities in literature, including here.
    //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calculus
  */

  The arithmetical operation that opposes zero to infinity is the division:
  divide by zero, you get infinity; divide by infinity, you get zero.
  So they're not opposites, they're inverses:

    R  =  1 / G    <=>    G  =  1 / R    <=>    R / G  =  G / R  =  1

  A mass of water or a copper wire have a Resistance.
  The air and your body have a Conductance.

  Assuming adequate conditions, an electric charge can flow through the air: it's called lightning.
  Your body is a dipole (go ask golfers).

  #define adequate_conditions

  Remember: the urge for a flow of electric charge to happen grows with the difference in electric potential.
  Ergo: greater difference  =>  greater charge flowing at once.
  In time domain, this instantaneous phenomenon translates as: higher flow rate.
  Whether this means electrons drifting faster or more electrons drifting is made irrelevant by the concepts of rate and charge.
  Which is precisely why these concepts are useful.

  Matter resists the flow. How much depends on its composition (Conductance / Resistance).
  Ergo: greater Resistance  =>  lower flow rate.

  This means:
    #  Assuming constant Resistance: the rate of the flow grows with the difference in potential.
    #  Assuming constant difference in potential: the rate of the flow decreases when the Resistance increases (and vice versa).
    #  Assuming constant rate of the flow: if the Resistance increases, the difference in potential must compensate by increasing too
         (and vice versa).

  For R to remain constant if flow and difference grow together (which they do), some mathematical formula must compensate for it
  in the definition of R.
  The difference is energy per charge. The flow: charge per time. They're not commensurable, so addition and subtraction are excluded.
  Exponentiation would require one of the variables to be dimension-less to make sense. No exponentiation excludes logarithms as well.
  We're left with multiplication and division. Multiplication is also excluded because we know that the two variables grow together.
  So if one increases so does the other, then so does the product, then R is not constant which is false by hypothesis.
  Division it is then. But which one is the numerator, which the denominator?
  We also know that for the flow to remain constant, the constraints that applied to it and the difference thus far now apply to R
  and the difference.
  If R is not the denominator it means that  R  =  flow * difference  --  which has been excluded (we don't know which ratio but we
  know it's a ratio... well actually now we know).

  If (R is not the denominator) is impossible, then it's false, then R is the denominator, then:

              difference in electric potential
    R  =  -----------------------------------------
           rate of the flow of the electric charge
  
  The flow of the electric charge is called Current. Its rate is called Intensity (of the current). Its usual symbol is I.
  The difference in potential is called Tension or Voltage. Its usual symbol is V in the English-speaking world, U otherwise.

  /*
    I personally tend to prefer U because V already stands for Volt, a common unit for Tension | Voltage.
    So if we use V for Voltage then the unit of V is V... I say slippery slope. However, I've also said "go play with fire"...
  */

  Ok, now that U and I have been granted convenient symbols maybe we can build some nice-looking set of equations:

           U                                         U             U * I
    R  =  ---    <=>    U  =  R * I    <=>    I  =  ---    <=>    -------  =  1
           I                                         R               R

  This set of equations is called Ohm's law.
  (You can check Wikipedia now.)

