  //  Amphibology of the current's intensity

  In English, the word current is usually used as a synonym of its own intensity.
  You'll often come across "I is the current expressed in Ampères".
  It's similar as if we were measuring "sound" in deciBels for lack of the word "amplitude".

  In French the physical quantity is called "courant" and its measure "intensité"
  (which can be challenging translation-wise).
  I've reproduced this semantics. Be aware that most authors won't.


  //  Social norms are here to help

  By convention, for historical reasons, current flows from positive to negative.
  Which is the opposite of what the electric field and electric charges actually do.
  I was tempted to use my own less confusing convention, but unfortunately this would only have been less confusing inside
  this book. If I give you tools that no one uses, you'll speak a language that no one understands. Not very helpful.
  What I did instead was allow current to make sense as long as I could.
  So remember (however impractical it is):

    Electric charge and Electric field  ->  minus to plus
                               Current  ->  plus to minus

