  Let's imagine the voltage between A and B is inferior to our new toy's breakdown voltage (we're below the threshold).
  The system is composed of two points with different electric potentials and an insulator. Nothing moves, no fun.
  Voltage increases enough and the toy becomes a conductor, current flows through it as if there was no toy.
  We've basically built a voltage-controlled switch... (Such things exist and this is not how they operate, try to forget this.)

  In its current state, it's not fun. But is it practical?
  A and B are points in space. There's a distance between them. Does our fragile insulator have to span the whole distance?
  Then its size would depend upon the distance between A and B. Wouldn't it affect its dielectric strength and make its behaviour
  unpredictable? -- Dielectric strength's dimension is voltage per distance so yes it would.

  Our insulator needs to span a fixed distance. It exists in a 3-dimensional space, so it's a volume.
  The simplest volume there is (mathematically speaking) is the parallelepiped.
  A parallelepiped is a stack of infinite rectangles (try to think "Calculus in god mode").
  Once the current crosses the longing-for-a-decent-name-new-toy, the electric field will fill each rectangle one by one in the
  direction of B.        //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno%27s_paradoxes#Paradoxes_of_motion
  
  If we're aiming for predictability, an identical field over the whole individual rectangle seems necessary in order to prevent randomness.
  So two faces of the fragile insulator must actually be conductors.
  Then (practically) ideal conducting cables will deal with the distance between A and B.

  Our new toy's been upgraded: it evolved from a fragile insulator to an insulator sandwich.
  The external slices are made of a conductive material.
  They're perfectly flat, parallel, and have the same shape as any section of the 3D solid.
  They don't in fact have to be rectangles.
  I'm clumsily trying to describe the shape for which I couldn't find any generic name.
  I'll name it a pseudo-prism and define it as such:
  
    A solid built by the translation of a start base up to an identical end base along an axis perpendicular to both bases.

  It could be a right prism of any base (trapezoidal, triangular, heptagonal, ...), a circular or oval cylinder, or its bases could be
  heart-shaped or random -- as long as the solid is an infinite stack of this shape along an axis perpendicular to both bases.

  Connecting our pseudo-prismatic sandwich to A and B would look like:

  A o---------
             |
    [conductive_base_α]
    [====insulator====]
    [conductive_base_β]
             |
  B o---------

  Let's define:
    U_A the electric potential of A
    U_B the electric potential of B
    U_α the electric potential of base α
    U_β the electric potential of base β
    U_bd the breakdown voltage of the insulator.
    Initial state -> U_α = U_β

  First case:    U_B - U_A  ≥  U_bd
    This is the easy one: an electrical breakdown will allow a current to flow through the insulator.
    The previously infinite Resistance will be broken to some finite value (R) and the system drawn by the schematic would be equivalent
    to a resistor between A and B. If we know R, Ohm's law gives us  I  =  (U_B - U_A) / R.
    The electric field travels from A to B, crossing the insulator. Equilibrium is achieved (U_B = U_A), the electrical breakdown ends.
    The total voltage is zero and the insulator is an insulator again.

  Second case:    U_B - U_A  <  U_bd
    In nodal analysis, an ideal cable is considered equivalent to a node (segment ≈ point), because the electric field is so fast that
    its displacement can be approximated as instantaneous for practical uses. Let's allow this assumption.
    The potential *energy* of A is transferred to α as soon as we connect our tripartite anonymous toy.
    But voltage is not energy, it's energy *per charge*. So U_A = U_α will take time.

    In our current example this time is undefined because it relies on I (intensity of the current = charge per time) which is inversely
    proportional to R and therefore relies (here) on a division by zero (since R = 0).
    Never mind, let's assume it's ok.

    Remember: if electrons can move, they will. Between A and α they can, so they will.
    The electric charge will accumulate on α. The initial potential energy at A is now kinetic energy in the ideal cable, progressively
    converting back into potential energy at α.
    U_α is increasing.

    Now let's break on through to the other side.
    Remember: the energy in one charge carrier is dependant upon the other charge carriers that surround it.
    So α charging will affect β. There will be an electric field across the insulator whose strength is dependant upon α's and β's surface
    and the distance between them: the more charge carriers are facing each other and the closer they are, the more energy per charge.
    Or, thinking backwards: the charge required to reach a certain voltage across the insulator grows with the bases' surface and proximity.

    So now there's a vector in α and an identical one in β.
    "Electrons can move" means (1) they're urged to and (2) the path is clear.
    The path between β and B satisfies both conditions.
    Then whatever happens at α will be reflected at B.
    Then α charging will result in β discharging in equal proportion: electrons will drift towards B.

    Full picture:
      # current between A and α
      # no current between α and β
      # current between β and B
    Until the electric potential of A has been transferred to α and the electric potential of B has been transferred to β.
    Which means that once the charge has finished drifting, the initial voltage between A and B is stored across the insulator.

  The bases α and β are called plates. The insulator is called dielectric. All three together form a condenser (or, more commonly,
  a capacitor).    //  "Condenser" is obsolete in English (except when talking microphones). "Condensateur" is still the French standard.

  The dielectric has a breakdown voltage U_bd dependant on its composition and its geometry.
  The plates have a maximum charge Q_max dependant on their geometry -- but also on the dielectric's breakdown voltage.
    Indeed, imagine there's room for x Coulombs (charge) on each plate, depending on the overall geometry this means χ Volts (voltage).
    If χ > U_bd it will never be reached: electrical breakdown will always happen first.
  Q_max determines how many charge carriers face each other at once. So a higher Q_max means more potential energy per charge which means
  more maximum voltage across the dielectric.  ->  Q_max  =  k * χ
  But χ might very well be unreachable, so U_bd is a more useful constant. Lets re-define:
    k  =  Q_max / U_bd

  The constant k seems quite useful. What would its dimension be?  ->  Charge per voltage.
  So "how much charge contributes to voltage" or "how much charge is needed to reach a certain voltage".
  Its actual name is Capacitance and its usual symbol is C. Q_max and U_bd are extreme cases, the general one is:

    C  =  q / U

    where q is the absolute value of the charge of either of the plates and U is the voltage across the dielectric.

